  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Gusty winds today, cold temperatures tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe low pressure system is centered over northern Quebec and \nwill continue to track ENE. As of this update the cold front has\nswept through the area. For Saturday, the remaining shortwave \nenergy behind the front should create some showers in the north.\nAny showers on the back side of the cold front will be in the \nform of isolated snow showers in the North Woods.\n\nIn addition, the approaching high pressure behind the exiting \nlow will tighten pressure gradients on Saturday. Winds gust are \nexpected to reach up to 35 mph, creating possible isolated power\noutages. Winds will relax Sunday.\n\nTemperatures will only rise a few degrees throughout the day on\nSaturday, and fall throughout the night as high pressure builds\nin and clouds clear. Temperatures will fall into the 20s across\nthe north and the 30s down to the coast. Lingering winds \novernight will combine with the cold temps to produce wind \nchills in the upper teens across the north and the 20s for the \nrest of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Generally dry weather Sunday through late next\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong surface high pressure will elongate cross the\nnortheastern United States beginning on Sunday and will \ngenerally control the weather through late next week. During \nthis time, several weak frontal passages will occur. However, \nwith only limited moisture available expect that the frontal \npassages will generally be dry. As the original high slides\neastward on Tuesday, additional ridging over southern Canada will\nmove into the region continuing the dry trend.",
